docs/pipeline/briefs/frame-write.md-207-  band. The Units table gains a `Year` column right after `#` (`K`, `1`, `2`, or `Level 1` and so on), units are
docs/pipeline/briefs/frame-write.md-208-  numbered through the band (unit 1 of year 2 follows the last unit of year 1), and each year is whole on its own:
docs/pipeline/briefs/frame-write.md-209-  its own units, its own end-of-unit assessments and its own mid-year and year-end course-level checks. The
docs/pipeline/briefs/frame-write.md-210-  **Days.** bullet gives the band's total first, then the days a year ("192: 64 a year for three years").
docs/pipeline/briefs/frame-write.md-211-  `frame.py` spreads the band's days over that many school years and notes the unit days by year. A subject page
docs/pipeline/briefs/frame-write.md-212-  that says "one frame per grade year" is met by this shape: each year's part of the file is that year's frame, and
docs/pipeline/briefs/frame-write.md-213-  the scope and sequence later writes one year at a time.
docs/pipeline/briefs/frame-write.md-214-- **Doses:** use the minutes, sessions a week and days a year the subject's STANDARD.md and APPROACH.md settle; never
docs/pipeline/briefs/frame-write.md-215-  the core 160-day yardstick unless the subject sets it. A smaller course means fewer minutes a week, never fewer
docs/pipeline/briefs/frame-write.md-216-  units (Cory 2026-11-15).
docs/pipeline/briefs/frame-write.md-217-- **Course-level checks:** grades K to 8 after-core checks do not take the core subjects' target weeks; place them
docs/pipeline/briefs/frame-write.md-218-  so they do not fall in a week (K to 5) where docs/courses/CALENDAR.md shows a core check for that grade, and say
docs/pipeline/briefs/frame-write.md-219-  which weeks.
docs/pipeline/briefs/frame-write.md-220-- **The seventh Units column** (lead 2026-11-25, from the pilot fix passes): arts heads it "Processes, anchors and
docs/pipeline/briefs/frame-write.md-221-  works", physical education "Cues and settings", health "Skill steps", SEL "Core link and routines", computer
docs/pipeline/briefs/frame-write.md:222:  science "Practices and concept areas", career education "Practices", ELD "Host unit and key language uses", library
docs/pipeline/briefs/frame-write.md-223-  "Inquiry phases".
docs/pipeline/briefs/frame-write.md-224-- **Owned standards cell:** a unit that owns no code (a carry-only or removable sensitive unit) says `None`; the
docs/pipeline/briefs/frame-write.md-225-  check reads `None` and an empty cell alike.
docs/pipeline/briefs/frame-write.md-226-- **A course-level check right after a unit** is that unit's end-of-unit assessment when it covers the unit, as in
docs/pipeline/briefs/frame-write.md-227-  the core frames; say so in the unit's row and the check's "What it checks" cell. Units not followed by a check end
docs/pipeline/briefs/frame-write.md-228-  in their own end-of-unit assessment.
docs/pipeline/briefs/frame-write.md-229-- **Grades 6 to 12:** the year-end check may sit in week 32, the finals week (CALENDAR.md stagger rules 3 and 4).
docs/pipeline/briefs/frame-write.md:230:- **After-core stagger:** each after-core course counts on its own in `frame.py calendar` (the five arts courses of a
docs/pipeline/briefs/frame-write.md-231-  band are five checks, not one). Writers keep checks off core check weeks; the lead runs one calendar pass across
docs/pipeline/briefs/frame-write.md-232-  the after-core frames for clashes among them.
docs/pipeline/briefs/frame-write.md-233-- **Gates the subject sets:** a subject page that holds building until a condition is met (ELD: C11 proven in the
docs/pipeline/briefs/frame-write.md-234-  core courses) still gets its frame now (Cory 2026-11-25: "please see if you can get them all completed"); the
docs/pipeline/briefs/frame-write.md-235-  frame says in **For the lead** which later stage the condition holds.
--
docs/ADOPTION-BAR-SUBJECTS.md-36-  reviewer, never by the builder, one from each third of the year: every lesson, page, tool and check in them. A
docs/ADOPTION-BAR-SUBJECTS.md-37-  course of four units or fewer is read whole. Below, "three units" means this sample.
docs/ADOPTION-BAR-SUBJECTS.md-38-- The subject part is read the same way: no criterion failed, and the part pass count above covers core and subject
docs/ADOPTION-BAR-SUBJECTS.md-39-  criteria together.
docs/ADOPTION-BAR-SUBJECTS.md:40:- **A small course is read the same way.** SEL and library skills courses may be smaller than other courses by
docs/ADOPTION-BAR-SUBJECTS.md-41-  the nature of the subject (decided 2026-11-11). They are "held to the whole core at the size its scope and sequence states":
docs/ADOPTION-BAR-SUBJECTS.md-42-  every criterion from C1 to C16 and every criterion of the subject part still applies, and what is measured is
docs/ADOPTION-BAR-SUBJECTS.md-43-  the number of units and days the scope and sequence itself sets out, not the size of a math or science course. Being small
docs/ADOPTION-BAR-SUBJECTS.md-44-  waives no criterion. **Smaller means fewer minutes a week, never fewer units** (Cory 2026-11-15, later.csv L43): each
docs/ADOPTION-BAR-SUBJECTS.md:45:  SEL and library course runs the whole year at one or two sessions a week, or about one every two weeks in the two library band courses (its frame states which), so every standard
docs/ADOPTION-BAR-SUBJECTS.md-46-  of its framework is still taught, practised spaced out over the year and checked. This fits how schools staff these
docs/ADOPTION-BAR-SUBJECTS.md-47-  subjects (a counselor or librarian seeing a class once a week).
docs/ADOPTION-BAR-SUBJECTS.md-48-- **Beyond the three units,** the reviewer reads: for C1, the whole coverage table, tracing ten standards of the
docs/ADOPTION-BAR-SUBJECTS.md-49-  reviewer's choice to lesson and check item; for C4, the progression page; for C8 and C9, one cumulative check;
docs/ADOPTION-BAR-SUBJECTS.md-50-  for C13, the approach page; for C3 and C16, all public copy for the course and the logic model.
