## Course-level checks

| Check | After unit | Check days | Respond days | What it checks |
|---|---|---|---|---|
| 1, mid-year | 2 | 1 | 1 | Week 13, one session, a written sitting, about 22 minutes for 30. It samples unit 1 on a new case: from a new topic and a short background passage, three research questions labeled thin or thick, one circled with its reason; three know-it, look-it-up or nobody-can items. Unit 2, which ended the week before, is not re-tested: its end-of-unit result is reused unscored, as a class count of the students who met each rubric step, written on this check's sheet. The respond day (week 14) reteaches the missed step in small groups from the sheet, ending with one fresh try |
| 2, year-end | 4 | 1 | 1 | Week 29, one session, a written sitting, about 25 minutes for 30. It samples units 1 to 3 on new cases: one research question and its thin or thick label (unit 1); key words, a synonym and a two-try search plan on a new catalog page, and two scenario cards (unit 2); three sources labeled primary or secondary with maker and purpose, and notes from a short passage into two given boxes (unit 3). Unit 4, which ended in week 28, is reused unscored as a class count on the sheet, as at mid-year. The respond day (week 30) reteaches the gaps in small groups from the sheet, then holds the year's reflect step: each student writes one thing they would like to learn more about and one step they now do well, and the class shares its products once more |

Placement: neither check falls in a week where docs/courses/CALENDAR.md shows a grade 3 core check (science 9, 20 and
30; math 12, 23 and 31; social studies 14, 25 and 28; ELA 16 and 32). The mid-year check sits in week 13, between math
(12) and social studies (14); the year-end check in week 29, between social studies (28) and science (30). Week 29 also
holds the health grade 3 year-end check; the after-core rule forbids only the core weeks, and any stagger among the
after-core courses is the lead's one calendar pass (For the lead, item 3).

