| # | Unit and compelling question | Days | Skills | Owned standards | Inquiry phases | What students learn | Why this unit, here | Check set adds | Sensitive topics and teacher notes |
|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|
| 1 | Questions worth researching. *What do we want to find out, and which questions can research answer?* | 5 | g3-research-questions | AASL.I.A.1 | Connect, wonder, express, reflect | After a short background read from a source the teacher gives, each student turns a class topic into three research questions of their own (AASL.I.A.1, owned here: "Turn a topic you care about or are studying into questions to research," checked alone), sorts them into thin (one fact) and thick (needs several sources) and picks one a source could answer; the class sorts a set of questions into "I know it", "I need to look it up" and "nobody can look this up". Begun here with help, owned later: deciding when a question needs research rather than a guess (AASL.IV.A.1, owned by g5-research-or-guess, grade 5). Out: narrowing a broad topic and a written plan (grade 4, g4-narrow-and-plan). Opening retrieval (day 2): one question-word question about a picture (g2-question-to-research). Days: show-me (hook, the cycle card with six steps, the thin and thick sort, modeled on the teacher's own topic), practice (sort six questions both ways; the look-it-up sort), inquiry (background read, three questions, pick one, find its answer in the source set and share it in pairs, the self-check), end-of-unit assessment, respond (it opens with the pair shares, about ten minutes, then the regrouped reteach). Skill card: "topic, three questions, thin or thick, pick one a source can answer"; withdrawn on the inquiry day. End-of-unit evidence (written sitting, about 20 minutes for 30): from a new topic and a short background passage, three research questions, each labeled thin or thick, one circled with a line on why a source could answer it; four one-answer sort items (know it, look it up, nobody can look it up). Tools: the cycle card, question sort cards, the spider background passage and six-source set (Curio Class writes all). Host: the inquiry questions of social studies grade 3 unit 1 (days 1 to 24), ss3-inquiry-questions, in the same weeks, on its town topic; stand-alone: "How do spiders catch their food?" | First because every later unit starts from a research question the student wrote; the first weeks are when social studies grade 3 also teaches compelling and supporting questions (one class question can serve both) | Candidate: the continuum's grade 3 background cell (a teacher-given source before the questions) at the inquiry day's opening; its grade 3 predict cell (what the new information may show) as one line on the question sheet | Neutral topic (spiders; the town's place is the host's question). A student who cannot think of a question may choose from three on a card. Whose voices: the unit records the makers of its passages and pictures (C12) |
| 2 | Searching safely. *How do we find a source for our question without getting lost or unsafe?* | 7 | g3-key-words; g3-safe-searching | AASL.VI.A.1 | Investigate (organization and search), reflect | Pulling the key words out of a research question, crossing out the small words, searching the invented printed catalog by key word, trying a synonym when nothing comes back, and recording each try on the search plan (question, key words, where to look, what came back, next try), the model introduced here; finding the book's area on a printed map of a library's main areas (fiction, nonfiction by subject number, reference). Using information and devices responsibly (AASL.VI.A.1, owned here: "Use information, devices and media responsibly when you learn," checked alone): the stop rule (close, tell, no blame), taking turns on a shared device, never typing a name, school, place or photo into a search or site, keeping a school password to oneself, and caring for books and devices. Begun here, owned later: searching more than one kind of source (AASL.IV.B.1, owned by g4-search-more-kinds, grade 4). Out: searching across kinds and comparing them (grade 4); changing a query on a results page and skipping ads (grade 5, g5-search-revise). Opening retrieval (day 2): find one book from a printed title entry (g2-catalog-lookup). Days: show-me (the stop rule and private information on scenario cards), show-me (key words and the search plan), practice (key-word searches in the printed catalog, a synonym when nothing comes back, the library map), practice (scenario cards in pairs: what to do and why; the turn routine on a pretend device card), inquiry (a search plan and a key-word search for the host question, finding the book's area on the map, the self-check), end-of-unit assessment, respond. Skill cards: "key words, search, look, try again" and "stop, close, tell, no blame"; withdrawn on the inquiry day. End-of-unit evidence (written sitting, about 24 minutes for 30): for a new research question, the key words, one synonym and a filled search plan using a new page of the invented catalog (rubric: key words fit, synonym tried, each try recorded); then four scenario cards, each answered with what to do and why (the safe-searching check). Tools: the invented catalog and results page, the library map, the scenario cards, the search plan sheet (Curio Class writes all). Host: a key-word search for the host question of ELA grade 3 unit 2 (days 40 to 77), the frogs research (e3-short-research), or for the science grade 3 unit 2 (days 17 to 33) magnets question; stand-alone: "What is on the far side of the moon?" | After unit 1 because a search needs a research question to pull key words from; before unit 3, which needs a found source in hand; safe searching sits with the first search so the stop rule is in place before any device day a school adds | Candidate: the continuum's grade 3 cells on bookmarked or teacher-chosen sites over free searching, as the reason the inquiry day uses a closed list; locating nonfiction at a readable level, as the second line of the search plan | The stop rule: the teacher page says what the adult does if an upsetting result ever appears on a screen (close it, the child tells, no blame) and points to health's steps if a child tells something worrying (APPROACH.md, Section field 1). No student types personal details, even on a pretend card. Searches use the closed catalog or a teacher-checked list only. Whose voices: the unit records the makers of its catalog entries and passages (C12) |
| 3 | Sources and notes. *Which sources tell us about stormy weather, and what do we keep from them?* | 6 | g3-source-kinds-and-makers; g3-notes-in-boxes | None | Investigate (sources and evidence), construct, reflect | In a teacher-chosen source set, telling primary sources (a photo, a weather log someone kept, a letter) from secondary ones (a book about it), and naming each one's maker and purpose, with help; when a source states something as plain truth, checking it in a second source from the set before using it. Making notes as key words, not copied sentences, in labeled boxes, one box per research question the student wrote or the teacher's boxes, and saying why each note goes in its box, with help; one credit line per source on the note sheet, as review (g2-credit-line). Begun here with help, owned later: questioning each source's maker and purpose before trusting it (AASL.IV.B.3, owned by g5-first-lateral-check, grade 5); choosing which sources to use (AASL.IV.A.3, owned by g5-choose-sources, grade 5); sorting notes by a plan one can explain (AASL.IV.B.4, owned by g4-evidence-notes, grade 4). Out: categories the student makes (grade 4, g4-evidence-notes); comparing what a primary and a secondary source can each tell (grade 4, g4-primary-secondary). Opening retrieval (day 2): pick the kind of source for two questions and name the maker of one invented page (g2-kinds-of-sources, g2-who-made-it); on the practice day, sort four fact cards into two given boxes (g2-fact-sort). Days: show-me (primary or secondary, maker and purpose), show-me (key-word notes in boxes), practice (sort a six-source set; a second-source check where two sources differ, recalling g2-two-books-differ; notes from one passage), inquiry (choose sources from the set for the host question, notes in boxes with a credit line each, a short search plan as review, a pair share and the self-check), end-of-unit assessment, respond. Skill card: "first-hand or about it, who made it, why; key words in the right box". End-of-unit evidence (written sitting, about 24 minutes for 30): a new four-source set on pond life: each labeled primary or secondary with its maker and purpose (one-answer items), one claim checked in a second source; then notes from one new passage into the student's two question boxes (rubric: key words not sentences, each note in a box that fits, a credit line). Tools: the storm source set (a photo with caption, a weather log, a letter, two book passages, a diagram), the pond set for the assessment, the note box sheet (Curio Class writes all). Host: the climates question of science grade 3 unit 6 (days 98 to 117) (s3-climates) where a school runs units out of order; in the usual weeks the unit runs on its stand-alone storm question, which keeps to weather; stand-alone: "What happens in a thunderstorm?" | After unit 2 because a found source is now in hand and has to be judged and noted; before unit 4, whose product is built only from notes; in weeks 15 to 20, beside social studies grade 3's primary and secondary sources | Candidate: the continuum's grade 3 cells on selecting evidence that answers the question with main ideas and details, and on asking questions of the text while reading, at the notes step; its media-accuracy cell as the photo-and-caption item | Social studies grade 3 unit 4 teaches primary and secondary sources on the first peoples of the region; library borrows only the idea and runs on weather cases, so no Native content enters this unit's case set (K to 5 neutral rule). Storms are kept to how they form and how people stay safe indoors; no injury or damage pictures. Whose voices: the unit records the makers of its sources (C12) |
| 4 | Research teams. *What can our team find out about pond life, and how do we share it well?* | 8 | g3-research-team; g3-make-product; g3-source-list; g3-gallery-feedback | AASL.III.D.1; AASL.II.A.1; AASL.I.B.3; AASL.I.C.1; AASL.I.C.2 | Investigate (with others), construct, express, reflect | In a research team each member finds facts on one subquestion, reports them aloud at the team meeting, and the team recorder writes who added what on the team sheet (AASL.III.D.1, owned here: "Speak up with ideas and findings when a research group talks"; AASL.II.A.1, owned here: "Notice and name what different classmates add to a shared piece of work"; both checked alone). Each student makes a product that answers their subquestion (a short report, a labeled diagram or a fact poster) using only facts from their notes, says whether the research answered the question or what is still unanswered (AASL.I.B.3, owned here: "Make a product that shows what your research taught you," checked alone), and ends it with a short source list: title, author, year and the kind of source, in the same order every time, from a model (review of the credit line, AASL.VI.B.2, owned by g2-credit-line, grade 2). In a gallery walk students read classmates' products and leave two notes on each: one thing the work taught them, in their own words, and one specific, kind suggestion tied to the skill card (AASL.I.C.1, owned here: "Read, watch or listen to classmates' work and respond to what it says"; AASL.I.C.2, owned here: "Give a classmate specific, kind advice that would make their work better"; both checked alone). Begun here, owned later: looking back on how a search went (AASL.I.D.4, owned by g5-search-log-look-back, grade 5), as the product's look-back line. Out: choosing which advice to use and a change log (grade 4, g4-revise-from-advice); a bibliography with web sources (grade 5). Opening retrieval (day 2): name what each of three sample team members added (g2-group-research); (day 3) a credit line for one cover (g2-credit-line) and say back what one sample poster teaches (g2-research-share). Days: show-me (the team meeting, subquestions, the recorder's sheet), inquiry (members find facts on their subquestions from the team source set, with notes in boxes as review, unit 3), team meeting day (each member reports; the teacher observes and scores the team skill, about 4 minutes a team, seven teams, about 28 minutes), show-me with practice (a product only from notes; the source list from the model card, then fixing two sample lists), inquiry (draft the product and its source list), gallery walk (the teacher models the two notes for five minutes, then the walk), respond to the notes (revise the product from them, the self-check), end-of-unit assessment. Skill card: "report, record, product from notes, source list, two notes". End-of-unit evidence (about 27 minutes for 30): the revised product is scored on its rubric (answers the subquestion, only facts from the notes, the answered-or-not line, the source list in order), with its draft day, the written gallery notes and the revision counted in the unit's days above; the written sitting, all at once, asks for a source list for three new covers in the model's order (about 8 minutes) and two gallery notes on a new sample product (about 12 minutes); the team skill is the team meeting day's observation. The unit's respond day is the year-end check's respond day (below). Tools: the team pond source set, the team sheet, the source list model card, gallery note slips (Curio Class writes all). Host: the speaking-reports work of ELA grade 3 unit 3 (days 81 to 119) (e3-speaking-reports) for the product, or the freshwater research of ELA grade 3 unit 4 (days 120 to 156); stand-alone: "What lives in a pond, and how do they depend on each other?" | Last because it gathers the year: a question (unit 1), a search (unit 2) and notes from judged sources (unit 3) are what each team member brings; the source list grows the note sheet's credit lines; and the gallery walk needs finished products to read | Candidate: the continuum's grade 3 cells on stating the main idea with supporting details and drawing a conclusion about whether the question was answered, at the product; presenting so the main points are clear, at the gallery; identifying what makes a good product, as the rubric read before drafting | Honest use without shaming (APPROACH.md, Sensitive ground, item 4): a student whose product copies a sentence is helped privately to put it in their own words and credit it, never named in front of the class. Gallery notes stay kind and about the work; the teacher reads the slips before they reach their owner. Teams are mixed by the teacher, never by background. Pond life keeps to plants and small animals; no predation pictures. Whose voices: the unit records the makers of its sources (C12) |

## Course-level checks

| Check | After unit | Check days | Respond days | What it checks |
|---|---|---|---|---|
| 1, mid-year | 2 | 1 | 1 | Week 13, one session, a written sitting, about 22 minutes for 30. It samples unit 1 on a new case: from a new topic and a short background passage, three research questions labeled thin or thick, one circled with its reason; three know-it, look-it-up or nobody-can items. Unit 2, which ended the week before, is not re-tested: its end-of-unit result is reused unscored, as a class count of the students who met each rubric step, written on this check's sheet. The respond day (week 14) reteaches the missed step in small groups from the sheet, ending with one fresh try |
| 2, year-end | 4 | 1 | 1 | Week 29, one session, a written sitting, about 25 minutes for 30. It samples units 1 to 3 on new cases: one research question and its thin or thick label (unit 1); key words, a synonym and a two-try search plan on a new catalog page, and two scenario cards (unit 2); three sources labeled primary or secondary with maker and purpose, and notes from a short passage into two given boxes (unit 3). Unit 4, which ended in week 28, is reused unscored as a class count on the sheet, as at mid-year. The respond day (week 30) reteaches the gaps in small groups from the sheet, then holds the year's reflect step: each student writes one thing they would like to learn more about and one step they now do well, and the class shares its products once more |

Placement: neither check falls in a week where docs/courses/CALENDAR.md shows a grade 3 core check (science 9, 20 and
30; math 12, 23 and 31; social studies 14, 25 and 28; ELA 16 and 32). The mid-year check sits in week 13, between math
(12) and social studies (14); the year-end check in week 29, between social studies (28) and science (30). Week 29 also
holds the health grade 3 year-end check; the after-core rule forbids only the core weeks, and any stagger among the
after-core courses is the lead's one calendar pass (For the lead, item 3).

## Cross-subject

Every lean is to a grade 3 core course, the same year, so each is on the availability calendar. Course days and weeks
for the other courses are as their frames state them and data/plans/calendar.csv places them; exact lesson-level
availability waits on their scopes and sequences, so each lean keeps its "if later" line. Library is not a core
course, so no core course assumes it (lesson 32).

