113: ## 4. What is left out, and why
114: 
115: - The AASL school librarian and school library frameworks: they describe adults and programs, not learners.
116: - Reading promotion and literature appreciation (the continuum's multiple literacies standard) beyond what a research
117:   unit needs: ELA owns reading; library units may use the same books.
118: - The continuum's design thinking strand: science and computer science own the design cycle; library leans on them
119:   and keeps only self-chosen making inside a research product (V.B.2, below).
120: - **By id.** The gate and bar criterion C1 work on ids, not themes. Each of the 68 is either excluded with a reason in
121:   `data/progressions/library-excluded.csv`, or owned once by a library row and carried as `~` in other years
122:   (section 3, Ownership); the owner row meets it through a research task (for example II.B.2, weighing
123:   perspectives, on the day two sources disagree; III.C.1, feedback, on the revise day of a research product).
124:   Carrying an id as `~` never stands in for its one owner row. Everything not placed below is met through a
125:   research task (Cory, 2026-11-21).
126: - **The eight ids checked against the second edition chart** (Cory, 2026-11-21, Desk q-fix-library-1: an id that
127:   belongs elsewhere goes to its receiving subject and is never dropped). Three stay in library, owned by a library
128:   row: III.A.1 (choosing to widen and deepen a question by researching with others: a group inquiry day), V.C.2
129:   (working out with others a new way to find something out, such as a class survey or an observation plan: an
130:   inquiry day) and V.B.2 (keeping going on a self-chosen making product through trial and error: the made product of
131:   a research unit). Five are placed in a receiving subject, listed in library-excluded.csv with that subject as the
132:   reason, and named there by the receiving subject's own page:
