148: ## 5. Cross-subject leans
149: 
150: **Who teaches what first.** ELA owns the research cycle and the writing of it (the model `research-cycle`; rows
151: `ek-shared-research` and `e1-shared-research` to `e12-research`), the list of sources in research writing (grade 4,
152: `e4-research`, which builds on library's grade 2 credit line and grade 3 source list), finding answers quickly across
153: several print and digital sources (grade 5, `e5-locate-sources`), source credibility in writing (grade 6, `e6-short-research`)
154: and a standard citation format (grade 7, `e7-research-questions`). Social studies owns sourcing and corroboration
155: (the models `source-sourcing` and `corroboration`, from the grade 2 rows `ss2-source-maker` and `ss2-two-accounts`)
156: and news reading (`ss8-news-literacy`, grade 8;
157: `us1-news-reading`, grade 11). Library owns what neither does: searching as a skill (changing a query, catalogs
158: and databases, choosing among results), leaving the page to check who is behind it (lateral reading, the first time
159: it is taught as a method: owned by a library row before grade 8, where `ss8-news-literacy` uses it on news), going upstream to the first source, honest use of others' work across media (not only in
160: writing), and choosing among tools. Where a library row meets an ELA or social studies skill it names that row's id in
161: `cross_subject` (for example `ela:e5-locate-sources`; never a model name, and never in `assumes`, section 3); the
162: progression says which came first in each grade. Re-read against ELA grades 3 to 7 on 2026-11-21
163: (docs/reviews/progression-library-2026-11-21.md): no conflict in the emphasis cells; two ownership lines above were
164: corrected and grade 3 note categories moved to grade 4.
165: 
166: - **ELA:** the research writing standards W.7 and W.8 from kindergarten and W.9 from grade 4, and informational
167:   reading.
168: - **Social studies:** C3 dimension 3 (sources and evidence) and Massachusetts news and media literacy (NML), from
169:   grade 8 (`ss8-news-literacy`). Library owns the lateral reading method; social studies uses it on its sources.
170: - **Science:** science rows obtain and combine information from texts, maps and media (for example `s2-water-where`,
171:   `s3-climates`, `s4-energy-resources`, `s5-protect-resources`; `s8-human-trait-technology` asks for reliable
172:   sources), but none teaches judging a source as a method. Library names such rows as host tasks and teaches the
173:   judging.
174: - **Computer science:** computer science owns how machine-made text tools work, in its own rows; library owns
175:   checking what they produce, and assumes no computer science row.

