e the fact with its credit line, put the book back in order; partner share; self-check), end-of-unit assessment, respond (it opens with the partner shares, about five minutes, then the regrouped reteach). Skill card: "look it up, copy the label, find the letter, find the number; title by author" pictured. End-of-unit evidence (about 13 minutes for 30): a sheet item: a printed catalog entry and a picture of a shelf of spines with call labels; the child circles the named book (g2-catalog-lookup, about 3 minutes); a sheet item: four spine cards numbered in shelf order (about 3 minutes); a sheet item: a credit line written for a new book cover (g2-credit-line, about 4 minutes, marked from the page: title and author, spelled so a reader can find the book); then a scanned pointing item: for a pretend case on a screen, point at the stop steps in order (about 2 minutes). Tools: catalog cards, call labels, spine cards, cover cards, the fossil read-aloud (Curio Class writes them all); an invented catalog screen for a school with one shared device. Host: a fossil book for ela grade 2 unit 2 (days 33 to 74); stand-alone: "What can a fossil tell us?" | After unit 1 because the class now knows the kind of source it wants and needs to find that book; the credit line joins it because the catalog card and the cover hold the same two things, title and author, and every product from here on ends with one. Before unit 3, which finds facts in two books and credits both | Candidate: the continuum's grade 2 cells on the library area that holds a topic (the call label's subject letters said aloud) and on knowing it is wrong to copy from an author or a peer, said with the credit line | Honest use without shaming (APPROACH.md, Sensitive ground, item 4): a product missing its credit line is fixed in the respond day's group, never named in front of the class. Borrowing follows the kindergarten rule (class book corner, no question about books at home, a lost book never a child's blame). Any device day follows the stop rule; the invented catalog screen names no product. Whose voices: the unit records the makers of its books and pictures (C12) |
