ge and one star and wish on a pretend page (g1-class-book, g1-star-and-wish, grade 1); pairs find a fact in a book, sort the facts, and draft the poster with credit lines), inquiry (each pair shares with another pair and says back what the other poster taught them; a short fix to the poster; the spoken look-back), end-of-unit assessment, respond (it opens with the posters on the wall and the self-check, about five minutes, then the regrouped reteach). Skill card: "plan two questions, ask, listen, bring it back; two facts, a picture, a credit line" pictured. End-of-unit evidence (about 17 minutes for 30): a sheet item: a pretend class's poster not seen before; each child writes one thing it taught them, from the poster (g2-research-share, about 4 minutes, marked from the page); then a station of three at the check table with a new topic card while the others finish poster fixes in the book corner: the group plans two questions to ask a person (questions on the topic, asked of a person, none about home or family) and each child names what one member added (g2-group-research, about 1.3 minutes a group, ten groups, about 13 minutes). Tools: the question frame, the poster template and poster paper, the pretend posters, the pollinator read-aloud (Curio Class writes them). Host: the pollination research of ela grade 2 unit 3 (days 78 to 118) (e2-research), or seeds and pollinators, science grade 2 unit 6 (days 60 to 65); stand-alone: "How do people at our school care for its plants?" | Last because it gathers the year: a question a source can answer (unit 1), the book found and credited (unit 2) and facts sorted and checked (unit 3) are what each pair brings to its poster, and asking a person adds the year's new kind of source (unit 1's "person who knows"). Placed in weeks 21 to 26 beside ELA's pollination research and social studies' interview unit (unit 6), so the class has met asking people and its question is real | Candidate: the continuum's grade 2 cells on a first sense of audience (who the poster is for, said before drafting) and on asking others to explain their ideas and offering one's own (the group's follow-up question) | Interviews (APPROACH.md, Sensitive ground, item 8): the topic stays on the school's plants or pollinators; questions are checked by the teacher before use; no one is asked about family history, home, beliefs, identity, health, money, food or meals, a family member's job, or status; any staff member stands in for a family member, and the family interview is optional and never a condition of the grade; answers are reported without a full name ("our school's gardener"). Groups are mixed by the teacher, never by background. Whose voices: the unit records whose work and words its sources and interviews show (C12) |

