cord". End-of-unit evidence (written sitting, about 22 minutes for 30): each student explains in writing the group's plan and why it answers the question (rubric); then three open questions for a new neutral topic, and a record of a short answer the teacher reads aloud once (rubric: questions open, record matches what was said). Tools: the survey and observation plan sheets, the tally sheet, the interview record sheet (Curio Class writes all). Host: an observation for the changing land unit of science grade 4 unit 4 (days 57 to 73), for example how rain moves soil in the schoolyard; stand-alone: "Which read-aloud book does our class most want to hear again?" | After unit 2 because students can now tell when the sources they judged do not hold an answer; the year's middle is when a class can run a week-long observation between sessions; before unit 4, where a student may use a survey or an asked adult for their own question | Candidate: the continuum's grade 4 cells on working with a group to research, decide and make a shared result, and on restating another's idea accurately before adding one's own, at the survey and the interview record | Surveys and asking people (APPROACH.md, Sensitive ground, item 8): questions stay on a neutral class topic and are checked by the teacher before use; no question about family, home, beliefs, identity, health, money, food or meals; surveys are of the class, with no names recorded; any school adult may be asked, inside school; the answers are reported without a full name. The observation is from a window or a set spot with the teacher. Whose voices: the unit records who answered (by role, never by name) (C12) |
