## Units

| # | Unit and compelling question | Days | Skills | Owned standards | C3 | What students learn | Why this unit, here | Check set adds | Sensitive topics and teacher notes |
|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|
| 1 | Where we live. "Why is our town where it is?" | 24 | ss3-map-skills; ss3-globe-grid; ss3-home-region; ss3-land-changes; ss3-inquiry-questions; ss3-claim-evidence | MA.3.T1.1; D2.Geo.1.3-5; D2.Geo.10.3-5; MA.3.T2.1; MA.3.T2.2; D2.Geo.2.3-5; D2.Geo.4.3-5; D2.Geo.5.3-5; D1.1.3-5; D1.2.3-5; D1.3.3-5; D1.4.3-5; D3.4.3-5; D4.1.3-5 | Owned: D2.Geo.1, 2, 4, 5, 10; D1.1 to D1.4; D3.4; D4.1 | Globe and flat map; continents and oceans reviewed; equator, prime meridian, hemispheres, a letter-and-number grid; why near the equator is warm and why rain also matters; their own town on a current map with title, legend, directions and scale (distances in whole bar lengths), its features and historic landmarks, and what one mapped landmark tells about the community's past; a map of the school grounds; their state among the fifty and every state of its US region located and named on a political map, with the region's rivers, lakes, coast and mountains and how they place towns; how people (Native nations among them) changed the land, from an early map beside a modern one, and from a short source how one group's culture shaped how it used and changed the land. The inquiry arc's first full turn (with each supporting question, the facts needed and one idea people could read two ways) and the first written claim; the first short biography read, opening with the readiness check on grade 1's leaders | Place comes first: every later unit puts people on this map, and units 3 to 7 use map-key, compass-directions and region from here. The arc and the claim are taught on the most concrete content of the year before harder history | Candidate: New York grade 3, a place's location by continent and hemisphere, practiced on world communities (feeds unit 3's comparison). Geography beyond the home state is already folded in by the outline's five-state read | Native nations named as present in the place today, not only as past land users. Local thread: no family addresses or home maps; maps use public places. Flagged for the sensitivity read |
| 2 | Who decides here. "Who should make the rules in our town?" | 24 | ss3-levels-of-government; ss3-rules-rights; ss3-taking-part; ss3-deliberation; ss3-state-constitution | MA.3.T1.3; D2.Civ.1.3-5; D2.Eco.12.3-5; D2.Civ.3.3-5; D2.Civ.4.3-5; D2.Civ.2.3-5; D2.Civ.6.3-5; D2.Civ.11.3-5; D2.Civ.7.3-5; D2.Civ.9.3-5; D2.Civ.10.3-5; MA.3.T6.5; D2.Civ.5.3-5 | Owned: D2.Civ.1 to 7, 9 to 11; D2.Eco.12. Practiced: D1, D3.4, D4.1 | Why schools, towns, states and the country have governments; the three levels and their leaders; how town and city governments differ; who governs the public schools (a school committee or board and its superintendent); taxes and fees paying for schools, roads, fire and water, and how local property taxes pay for schools; tribal nations as governments; why rules and laws exist, a school rule and a town law traced to the problem each solves; rights with responsibilities; voting, meetings, boards, volunteering, and one benefit and one difficulty of working together in a family, a school, a workplace, a volunteer group and a government; disagreeing respectfully, ending in a class vote on a real class question with the minority heard first; what a constitution is (a basic plan that sets up a government, says what it may and may not do, and may list protected rights), taught before the comparison; every state's constitution, with two or three things found in their own, where it came from and what its parts do, compared with the US Constitution's three parts (named as a preview: lawmakers, a leader, judges) and with one other system (a tribal nation's or another country's) | Civics follows place: the levels of government are the town, state and country students just mapped. The grade 1 civics rows are dormant across grade 2, so the unit opens on readiness checks. ss3-state-constitution stays here (settled by the expert read): the levels just taught are the frame a state constitution sets up. The US Constitution is met here as the other plan in the comparison; unit 6 reviews what a constitution is for, now for the national plan, and completes D2.Civ.5 with where the US Constitution came from | Candidate: California grade 3, the branches of government and the governments of tribal nations in the local region (settled at the scope and sequence, 2026-11-14: kept as the leaders at each level and the branches of the state and national governments; the town's own form studied as it is, and a tribal government described from its own constitution, with no three-branch template assumed); Tennessee's grade 3 civics read at the unit plan | The class vote is on a real class choice, never a contested public issue picked by the course. State constitutions read from official state pages; Massachusetts's 1780 constitution is one worked example among several states |
| 3 | Making a living here. "How does our community get what it needs?" | 20 | ss3-goods-services; ss3-scarcity-choice; ss3-money-saving; ss3-world-trade | D2.Eco.3.3-5; D2.Eco.7.3-5; D2.Eco.1.3-5; D2.Eco.2.3-5; D2.Eco.5.3-5; D2.Eco.9.3-5; D2.Eco.10.3-5; D2.Eco.14.3-5 | Owned: D2.Eco.1, 2, 3, 5, 7, 9, 10, 14. Practiced: D1, D3.4, D4.1 | Goods and services, producers and consumers; natural, human and capital resources (a bakery's wheat from a farm, its bakers and its ovens); profit and pricing; scarcity, costs and benefits, opportunity cost, incentives; money against barter; banks, credit unions and insurance, interest as the price of using money, a rate shown as dollars a year on each $100 saved and compared across two made-up banks (no percent); a made-up class savings plan tracked over weeks; what is made here and what comes from far away, read from labels and a world map; one comparison with a present-day market town in Ghana | Community economics in all five states the outline read, not in the anchor's grade 3. It follows civics because unit 2's taxes and services set up the question of who pays for what, and it needs unit 1's world map for trade | Candidate: California grade 3, specialization and the division of labor in the local economy, and how the local economy changed (the second part feeds unit 7) | All amounts are made-up class examples; no student shares family money facts. The country is Ghana (settled by the expert read: no earlier grade fixes a country, and grade 4 studies Canada and Mexico); the unit plan keeps the present-day market town apart from ancient Ghana, which grade 6 teaches (ss6-west-africa-trade) |
| 4 | The first peoples of our land. "How have the Native nations of our region shaped this place, then and now?" | 18 | ss3-first-nations-region; ss3-primary-secondary | MA.3.T2.3; MA.3.T2.3a; MA.3.T2.3b; MA.3.T2.3c; MA.3.T2.3d; D2.Geo.6.3-5; D2.His.12.3-5 | Owned: D2.Geo.6; D2.His.12. Practiced: D1, D3.4, D4.1 | The Native nation or nations of the student's own region, past and present, beside five worked cases from grade 4's five regions: the Penobscot Nation (Northeast; the Wampanoag nations are met in kindergarten, in unit 5 and in grade 5, so the worked case is another nation), the Cherokee Nation (Southeast homelands, with its present lands in Oklahoma told apart from them; met in grade 2 as a forced move, this unit adds the nation's homelands, ways of life and government), the Menominee Indian Tribe of Wisconsin (Midwest), the Navajo Nation (Southwest) and the Makah Tribe (West), each one nation, not a stand-in for its region; at least three named nations met by every student (MA.3.T2.3a); their own names, homelands and tribal lands today (MA.3.T2.3a and b in general form), how rivers, coasts and land, and each nation's ways of life, placed towns and seasonal camps (3.T2.3c, D2.Geo.6), foods, crafts, words, place names and knowledge given to the wider country, starting from a nation near the school (3.T2.3d), and that these nations govern themselves today (sovereignty, defined as a nation's right to govern itself). Telling a primary source from a secondary one by how it relates to the question: a nation's oral history, an object, a museum label, a textbook page; asking questions of two or three sources on one topic. Events are placed as before and after, then and now; the timeline itself waits for unit 5 | Native nations come before first meetings (unit 5) so students know who was here, and that they are here now, before Europeans arrive in the story. The primary and secondary sort is taught here, on the nations' own materials, because unit 5's colonial sources need it | Candidate: California grade 3, how tribal nations' governments relate to federal and state governments today, at grade 3 depth | Native content: the fresh sensitivity agent's read, after it studies the nations' own pages named in the course brief. Taught from each nation's own published materials; sacred or restricted knowledge not taught; no costumes, headdresses or crafts copying sacred objects; the class finds whose land the school is on from the nations' own pages. Each worked case starts from the nation's own public pages, and the unit plan confirms each case against them |
| 5 | First meetings and early colonies. "Could the colonists and the Native nations have shared the land?" | 32 | ss3-timeline; ss3-first-meetings; ss3-early-colonies; ss3-colonial-daily-life; ss3-slavery-colonies; ss3-sea-trade | D2.His.1.3-5; MA.3.T3.1; MA.3.T3.2; MA.3.T3.3; MA.3.T4.1; MA.3.T4.1a; MA.3.T4.1b; MA.3.T4.1c; MA.3.T5.1; MA.3.T5.2; MA.3.T5.3; D2.His.2.3-5; MA.3.T5.4; MA.3.T5.5; MA.3.T5.5a; MA.3.T5.5b; MA.3.T5.5c; D2.Geo.7.3-5 | Owned: D2.His.1, 2; D2.Geo.7. Practiced: D2.His.12 (unit 4), D1, D3.4, D4.1 | A class timeline in years, decades and centuries; why Europeans sailed west in the 1500s and 1600s as causes and effects; explorers' routes on more than one coast; one explorer's description of a Native nation beside what that nation says of itself; an early 1600s map beside a modern one. Two or three early colonies and their Native neighbors, taught in the order they were founded (for example St. Augustine and the Timucua, Jamestown and the Powhatan, Plymouth and the Wampanoag): why people came, how they crossed the Atlantic (the Mayflower's voyage as one case), the Mayflower Compact as self-government (3.T4.1a), families' hardships (3.T4.1b), first contacts and later relations (Samoset and Massasoit; the Wampanoag and Abenaki/Wabanaki), and the harvest-feast story from the record and from Wampanoag people today (3.T4.1c; the 1621 harvest was met in kindergarten, and this unit adds the colony's founding, its self-government and the land loss around it); colonial and Native leaders' choices compared, one pair per colony (Bradford and Massasoit; John Smith and Wahunsenaca; Menendez de Aviles and a Timucua leader named in the record); land loss, disease, conversion (some colonists pressed Native people to give up their own beliefs and ways, for example the praying towns, told with what the nations say of it) and war, with the 1622 war between the Powhatan nations and Jamestown as the conflict case from both sides, and King Philip's War named as a second case only if the unit plan's days allow (grade 5 teaches it). Daily life from paintings, tools, houses and short texts, servants and enslaved people included. Slavery legal in every colony. Fishing and shipbuilding, with barter where coin was scarce (3.T5.5a), Atlantic and Caribbean trade with the Triangular Trade named plainly (3.T5.5b), and port towns in more than one colony in place of Massachusetts's seaports (3.T5.5c) | Time order: after the nations of unit 4, before the founding of unit 6. It needs unit 1's maps and compass directions, unit 4's primary and secondary sort, and starts the timeline that units 6 and 7 use. The largest unit, because it carries six rows and most of the year's history content (merged from two outline units on purpose) | Candidate: a Curio Class context addition (no Tennessee grade 3 requirement claimed), French and Dutch colonies (New France, New Netherland) named on the map so the colonies are not only English and Spanish | Hard history: disease, war and land loss named plainly; slavery taught as legal and cruel, with enslaved people resisting and building lives, people named as enslaved people; the trade in people named as part of the economy. No reenactment of the feast, of conflict or of slavery; no role play or simulation. Enslaved people's own words where they exist. Conversion told plainly, as the nations tell it, with no religion judged. Native and hard-history content flagged for the sensitivity read |
| 6 | Founding ideas. "What does it mean that government needs the consent of the governed?" | 12 | ss3-founding-ideas | MA.3.T6.3; MA.3.T6.4; D2.Civ.8.3-5 | Owned: D2.Civ.8. Practiced: D2.Civ.5 (completed: where the US Constitution came from, set beside unit 2's state constitution), D2.His.1 (timeline), D1, D3.4, D4.1 | Why colonists felt treated unfairly and declared independence, in brief; what the Declaration says about rights and consent; what a constitution is for, reviewed from unit 2, now for the national plan, and that the Bill of Rights lists rights; that the founding documents promised rights, but laws and practice denied many people equal rights and a voice: voting rules differed by state and mostly favored adult white men, often only those with property, some women and free Black men voted in New Jersey until 1807, enslaved people and Native nations were left out; citizenship, voting and equal rights as different questions; national symbols, songs and holidays, why each was made, from the record, and what they stand for. The Revolution's events are left to grade 5 | Follows the colonies in time and closes the early history on one timeline. Unit 2's state constitution and the national plan's three parts are reviewed here, and D2.Civ.5 is completed for the national plan. Short because the events are taught in grade 5, which picks this row up across grade 4 | Candidate: California grade 3, national landmarks and essential documents (the Liberty Bell, the Statue of Liberty) beside the symbols | Who was left out stated plainly. Patriotic songs and holidays taught as what they mean and why they were made; no student is required to perform a patriotic act (West Virginia State Board of Education v. Barnette, 1943; values rule a). Flagged for the sensitivity read |
| 7 | How our community changed. "Who has made our community what it is?" | 20 | ss3-community-history; ss3-biography; ss3-informed-action | MA.3.T1.2; MA.3.T6.1; D4.3.3-5; D2.His.3.3-5; D4.2.3-5; D4.7.3-5; D4.8.3-5 | Owned: D2.His.3; D4.2, 4.3, 4.7, 4.8. Practiced: D1, D2.His.1, 12, D3.4, D4.1 | The local history project: who has lived in the community over time (the Native nation or nations, founders, enslaved and free workers, immigrants and newcomers today), why people move, local celebrations and traditions, from interviews with family and neighbors, old photographs and a historical map of their own state: how far the state, colony or territory reached at one date and why its first towns grew where and when they did (a river, a harbor, a road or railroad, a mine), in general form since a western state's 1700s may be a Native or Spanish story; what changed and what stayed; findings shared with someone outside the class. Each student writes a short biography of a person who shaped the community, in time order with details from at least two sources. The year's informed action: first, how one person alone and one group together took on a regional or world problem (a short given case) and what came of each; then a local problem from the project, ways to help weighed, alone and together, with a prediction for each, a fair decision, one contribution | The year ends where it began, at home, now with every tool of the year: maps (unit 1), government (unit 2), the local economy (unit 3), the nations (unit 4), sources and the timeline (units 4 and 5). Informed action comes last because it rests on the project's evidence | Candidate: California grade 3, the local community's history from its first peoples to today, and its economy over time (matches; confirms the unit) | No student is asked to share family immigration or legal status; families choose the interview questions. Enslaved and free workers in the town's history named plainly. The action is the class's evidence-based choice, never a cause the course picks; the class chooses its problem afresh and builds on grade 2's storm-readiness work only if it chooses to. Flagged for the sensitivity read |

