 english-language-arts-grade-3
Unit 1 Reading against the odds. "How do people get past what keeps them from books and school?" days 39 skills e3-text-questions; e3-central-message; e3-character-actions; e3-pictures-and-words; e3-reading-range; e3-main-idea; e3-literal-nonliteral; e3-word-strategies; e3-long-words; e3-fluency; e3-informative; e3-writing-tasks; e3-writing-process; e3-discussion; e3-conventions learn Anchors: three original Curio Class picture-book stories set today, each in a different place, of children who work to reach books and school (a girl whose library comes by boat; a boy who walks to a once-a-week book bus; a girl who learns to read beside her grandmother), 300 to 800 words, measure targets about 420 to 620; a short set of fables and folktales of learning from named cultures (300 to 600 words); an informational set on how books reach children (a paired original article on book carriers today and an anchor article on the 1930s packhorse librarians of Kentucky, built on public-domain federal records and photographs); a short Curio Class retelling of how Booker T. Washington learned to read, with a few lines from his own 1901 account, opened by one or two plain sentences on what slavery and freedom meant for him. Asking and answering questions with the place in the text named (RL.3.1, RI.3.1); recounting each story and stating its message with the details that carry it (RL.3.2, fables and folktales here, myths in unit 2); describing a character by traits tied to actions and how the actions drive events (RL.3.3); what illustrations add to mood and character, and placing each story on a world map from its pictures and words (RL.3.7, RI.3.7); main idea and key details of the informational set, of a text read aloud, and of a short recorded library presentation with a labeled bar graph of books lent each month (numbers within grade 2 math), told from the words, the picture and the numbers together (RI.3.2, SL.3.2); words and phrases as used in the stories, literal and nonliteral meaning told apart (RL.3.4, L.3.5a; real-life connections, 5b, and shades of meaning, 5c, continued on the unit 2 poems); a strategy chosen for each unknown word: context, a known affix, a known root, a glossary or dictionary (L.3.4a to d); the independent reading system launched (log, conference, book choice). Writing: the daily exit ticket (W.3.10) and an informative piece, one paragraph growing to two, on why getting books is hard in some places, with grouped facts, linking words and a conclusion (W.3.2a to d, W.3.4); a reading contract drafted, critiqued against one look-for and revised with peer and teacher feedback, and the reading-strategy bookmark planned and published (W.3.5: planning, revising and editing taught here; using technology to produce and publish, W.3.6, waits for keyboarding in unit 2). Fluency: a passage from a unit 1 story reread for accuracy, rate and expression and confirmed in context (RF.3.4a to c), then recorded as audio read for listeners (SL.3.5); poetry read aloud is added in unit 2. Talk: the discussion routine and norms (SL.3.1a to d), asking a speaker questions (SL.3.3), speaking in complete sentences when the discussion asks for detail (SL.3.6 in its grade 2 form; owned in unit 3). Skills block from day 1: the long-word screen, then the sequence (RF.3.3a prefixes and derivational suffixes with meanings first; RF.3.3b Latin suffixes from unit 2, RF.3.3c multisyllable words from unit 3, RF.3.3d the year's irregular words all year); spelling on the same patterns, possessives (L.3.2d), suffix spelling and dictionary checks (L.3.2e to g); dialogue punctuation (2c) comes in unit 2, titles (2a) in unit 3, commas in addresses (2b) in unit 4. Retrieval: grade 2's wh-questions and simple and compound sentences; the fall oral reading screen is diagnostic (warm-ups). The unit's word list, studied and spelled. Lettered parts of these standards taught in another unit: L.3.2a (unit 3); L.3.2b (unit 4); L.3.2c, L.3.5b, L.3.5c (unit 2).
