 science-grade-3
Unit 1 Pushes, pulls and swings. "What makes a swing start, stop and keep coming back?" Anchor: a playground swing (and a class pendulum of string and washers) that one push sets going, that swings in a pattern we can predict, and that two equal pushes from opposite sides hold still days 16 skills s3-balanced-forces; s3-motion-patterns learn Pushes and pulls have a strength and a direction; balanced forces (equal pushes on a box from both sides) give no change in motion and an unbalanced force starts, stops, speeds up or turns an object; gravity named as a pull toward the ground. The force arrow drawing, revised as tests come in. A fair test, first as a class and then in small groups: how many pushes, how strong compared with another, which direction, one change at a time. Motion that repeats (the swing, a pendulum, a ball rolling back and forth in a bowl, a see-saw) recorded in a table so the pattern shows; the pattern used to predict where the object will be next, and a failed prediction read as a sign something changed. The weather log starts in week 1
Unit 2 Pushes and pulls without touching. "How can things push and pull without touching, and how can we put that to work?" Anchor: a steel paper clip on a thread that hangs in the air, leaning toward a magnet it never touches, and a balloon rubbed on hair that lifts bits of paper days 17 skills s3-magnet-static; s3-design-problem learn Students ask testable questions about pulls and pushes between objects that do not touch and investigate them: magnet to magnet, magnet to paper clips, one magnet against two, distance, and turning a magnet around (attract becomes repel); static electricity with a balloon on hair and paper bits. Force arrows drawn between objects that are not touching. Then a design problem a magnet can solve, written as need, criteria and constraints (materials, time, cost) precisely enough that two solutions could be judged against it; a brief comparison shows how a changed need (the cupboard now opened by a younger class) changes the criteria, the 3-5-ETS1-1 element on changing human needs and wants. The clarification's electromagnet example as a teacher demonstration: a nail wound with wire on one D cell picks up paper clips, switched on and off; students observe and ask questions, and build no circuit (circuits are grade 4). The named problem: a magnetic latch that keeps the class supply cupboard door shut but opens with one hand; the second clarification example (keeping two moving toy cars from touching) is the transfer item. Students may sketch and try one solution
Unit 3 Growing up and taking after. "How do living things grow and change, and why do they look like their parents but not exactly?" Anchor: the class's own plants, grown from one packet of seeds sown on planting day (science day 18): they come up looking like the picture of the parent plant but not quite like each other; and photographs of one kind of hydrangea bush with blue flowers in one garden and pink in another (the soil decides the color) days 22 skills s3-life-cycles; s3-inherited-traits; s3-environment-traits learn Life cycles watched first-hand (the fast-cycling plants or radishes sown on planting day, and mealworms becoming darkling beetles) and modeled as drawn cycles and sequenced cards; compared with other organisms' cycles (a butterfly, a frog, a flowering plant) to find that every cycle differs in stages and timing yet all share birth, growth, reproduction and death; the model revised when observation shows a missed stage. Traits: photos, tallies and bar graphs of puppies in a litter, seedlings from one packet and ear length in rabbits show offspring are like their parents and siblings vary. Then the environment, opened by one retrieval task on grade 2's light-and-water test: the class crowding test (the same seeds sown close together and spread out, the rest kept the same; crowded plants stay smaller) and other cases (the hydrangea whose soil decides its flower color, plants stunted by too little water, a dog fed too much and walked too little, flamingos fed less shrimp turning paler) support the explanation that the environment can change a trait; students sort traits into inherited, shaped by the environment, or both
Unit 4 Surviving where you live. "Why do some living things survive in a place while others do not?" Anchor: the Arctic tundra in winter: musk oxen form a ring against wolves, white-coated hares are hard to see on the snow, and no rainforest tree frog or cactus could live there days 20 skills s3-animal-groups; s3-variation-advantage; s3-habitat-survival learn An argument with claim, evidence and reasoning that some animals form groups that help members survive: wolves hunt in packs, fish school, bees divide work, musk oxen circle; evidence from video, hunting success given as counts out of 10 hunts alone and in a group read from a bar graph (the unit plan takes the counts from its source; no percent), and readings; the argument admits that some animals live alone and survive in other ways. Variation: the class camouflage hunt (paper "moths" of several colors on a patterned cloth, found by "bird" classmates) gives data that the better-hidden survive more often; plants with bigger thorns are eaten less; a sourced case in one kind of bird whose brighter-colored males attract mates more often (the mates case, with the data students explain; no claim that brighter animals always do); a small difference changes who survives, finds mates and has young. Habitat: in a given habitat some organisms survive well, some less well and some not at all (a cactus in a desert and in a bog; in the Arctic, the polar bear survives well, the wood frog survives partial freezing in winter and thaws in spring, and a rainforest tree frog could not survive at all; each judged from evidence of the organism's needs and characteristics and the place's conditions, never from the animal's group, and the wood frog not labeled as surviving less well just because its trait is special; the wood frog links back to ELA grade 3's frog unit), argued from the organisms' needs and the habitat's conditions, with organisms and habitat as one system
Unit 5 Environments change. "How do we know places have changed, and what happens to living things when they do?" Anchor: fossil tree stumps of an early kind of tree, preserved where they grew in rock in the Arctic on Svalbard, Norway, from a time when that land lay near the equator; no such forest grows there now (a teacher may swap in a fossil found in the class's own state); the sea fossil on a mountain is grade 6 unit 4's anchor, so it is not used here days 16 skills s3-fossils; s3-environment-change-solution learn Fossils by kind, size and where they are found as evidence of past organisms and environments: sea shells on dry land show sea once covered it (worked case: the fossil whales in the desert of Wadi Al-Hitan, Egypt), tropical plant fossils in the Arctic show a once-warm climate, fossils of extinct organisms show living things have changed; older fossils lie deeper in undisturbed layers, so ages are relative. Nature of science: explanations of the past come from evidence and change when new evidence arrives. Then the present: when an environment changes (a new highway through a forest, a dam on a river, a fire) some living things survive, some move and some die; students make a claim about the merit of a solution judged against the criteria language of unit 2. Worked case: a wildlife crossing over a highway; second case: a fish ladder at a dam; transfer: replanting after a fire. Two short cases complete the clarification's kinds: a stream warmed when the shading trees on its bank are cut (temperature) and a new plant that spreads and crowds out the food of local animals (other organisms); with the highway (land), the dam (water) and the fire (food), each kind has a case, one change at a time
Unit 6 Weather, climate and hazards. "What weather can we expect here, and how can people get ready for the dangerous kind?" Anchor: the class's own weather log since the first week: no two days alike, yet each season has its own kind of weather, and, this year or last, the town had a weather warning (from the National Weather Service's record for the county if the class has not seen one) days 20 skills s3-weather-data; s3-climates; s3-weather-hazard learn The class log and local records in tables, pictographs and bar graphs: monthly average temperature, rain or snow, and wind direction, to describe the typical weather of each season; averages are supplied, not calculated; one day's weather is not a season's. Climate (the pattern over many years) told from weather (today), from texts, maps and data: warm near the equator and cold near the poles, wet and dry places; the named comparison: our town, a rainforest city near the equator (Manaus, Brazil), a desert city (Cairo, Egypt) and a polar town (Utqiagvik, Alaska). Then a claim about the merit of designs that reduce a weather hazard's impact, judged against criteria and constraints: the three the standard names (a barrier against flooding, a wind-resistant roof, a lightning rod), with a quick model test of a roof that stays on under a fan as the class's evidence. People cannot stop severe weather but can lessen its harm
