Unit 1 Where we live. "Why is our town where it is?" days 24 skills ss3-map-skills; ss3-globe-grid; ss3-home-region; ss3-land-changes; ss3-inquiry-questions; ss3-claim-evidence learn Globe and flat map; continents and oceans reviewed; equator, prime meridian, hemispheres, a letter-and-number grid; why near the equator is warm and why rain also matters; their own town on a current map with title, legend, directions and scale (distances in whole bar lengths), its features and historic landmarks, and what one mapped landmark tells about the community's past; a map of the school grounds; their state among the fifty and every state of its US region located and named on a political map, with the region's rivers, lakes, coast and mountains and how they place towns; how people (Native nations among them) changed the land, from an early map beside a modern one, and from a short source how one group's culture shaped how it used and changed the land. The inquiry arc's first full turn (with each supporting question, the facts needed and one idea people could read two ways) and the first written claim; the first short biography read, opening with the readiness check on grade 1's leaders
